window.SUBJECTS = window.SUBJECTS || [];
window.SUBJECTS.push({"id":"geo","n":"Geography","tab":"Geography","w":"Earth, physical geography, India, resources, population and the world.","topics":[{"id":"earth","n":"Earth and the Solar System","qs":[{"id":"q0061","q":"Which of the following statements best describes the position of the North Temperate Zone?","o":["Between the Arctic Circle and North Pole","Between the Tropic of Cancer and Arctic Circle","Between the Equator and Tropic of Cancer","Between the Equator and Arctic Circle"],"a":1,"x":"The North Temperate Zone lies between the Tropic of Cancer (23½°N) and the Arctic Circle (66½°N).","e":"Torrid zone: between the two Tropics. Frigid zones: beyond the Arctic and Antarctic Circles.","src":["ASO 18 Aug 2025 S2"]},{"id":"q0108","q":"If the Earth's axis were not tilted, what change would most likely occur regarding the temperature pattern?","o":["The temperature at the poles would increase","Equatorial regions would receive slanting rays","Tropical regions would freeze","Seasonal variation would be less pronounced"],"a":3,"x":"Seasons exist because Earth's axis is tilted 23½°. With no tilt, each place would get the same sun angle all year, so seasons would almost disappear.","e":"That is why the Tropics of Cancer and Capricorn sit at 23½° north and south.","src":["ASO 18 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0119","q":"Why does a lunar eclipse NOT occur every full moon?","o":["The Earth rotates too quickly","The Earth does not always cast a shadow","The Moon becomes invisible during full moons","The Moon’s orbit is tilted relative to the Earth’s orbit"],"a":3,"x":"The Moon's orbit is tilted about 5° to Earth's orbit, so at most full moons the Moon passes above or below Earth's shadow. An eclipse happens only near the nodes.","e":"A lunar eclipse can happen only at full moon; a solar eclipse only at new moon.","src":["ASO 18 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0141","q":"Which of the following best explains why the equatorial regions are warmer?","o":["Equator has lower atmospheric pressure.","Equatorial regions have more deserts.","Equator is closer to the sun.","Equatorial regions receive vertical rays of the sun."],"a":3,"x":"Near the equator the Sun's rays fall almost vertically, so the same energy is concentrated on a smaller area. That makes equatorial regions warmer.","e":"Near the poles the rays are slanting and spread over a larger area.","src":["ASO 19 Aug 2025 S2"]},{"id":"q0185","q":"The difference between sidereal and solar day on Earth is primarily due to the _______.","o":["Earth’s orbital motion around the Sun","Earth’s elliptical orbit","moon's gravitational pull","seasonal changes in the atmosphere"],"a":0,"x":"A sidereal day (about 23 h 56 min) is one rotation against the stars. A solar day is about 4 minutes longer because Earth also moves along its orbit and must turn a bit more to face the Sun again.","e":"Over a year this adds up to one extra rotation: about 366 sidereal days in 365 solar days.","src":["ASO 19 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0280","q":"What is the shape of the Earth?","o":["Geoid","Oval","Round","Circular"],"a":0,"x":"Earth is a geoid, 'earth-shaped': roughly a sphere, flattened at the poles and bulging at the equator.","e":"The equatorial diameter is about 43 km more than the polar diameter.","src":["ASO 20 Aug 2025 S2"]},{"id":"q0427","q":"Between the orbits of which planets are asteroids found?","o":["Mars and Jupiter","Earth and Mars","Saturn and Jupiter","Mercury and Venus"],"a":0,"x":"The asteroid belt lies between the orbits of Mars and Jupiter; Ceres, a dwarf planet, is its largest body.","e":"Order: Mercury, Venus, Earth, Mars, [asteroid belt], Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus, Neptune.","src":["ASO 22 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q1611","q":"About ______ percent of the Earth's surface is water-covered.","o":["71","70","73","75"],"a":0,"x":"About 71% of Earth's surface is covered by water; land is about 29%.","e":"Oceans hold about 97% of Earth's water; freshwater is about 3%.","src":["Patwari 20 Aug 2022 S2"]},{"id":"q1615","q":"Which of the following are the small pieces of rocks that move around the sun?","o":["Planets","Galaxy","Meteoroids","Constellations"],"a":2,"x":"Meteoroids are small rocky pieces moving around the Sun; one burning in Earth's atmosphere is a meteor (shooting star), and one reaching the ground is a meteorite.","e":"Asteroids are larger rocky bodies, mostly between Mars and Jupiter.","src":["Patwari 18 Sep 2022 S1"]},{"id":"q1394","q":"Which of the following eras is considered as the age of the dinosaurs?","o":["Cenozoic","Precambrian","Palaeozoic","Mesozoic"],"a":3,"x":"The Mesozoic Era (about 252–66 million years ago: Triassic, Jurassic, Cretaceous) is the 'Age of Dinosaurs'; it ended with an asteroid-linked mass extinction.","e":"Cenozoic: 'Age of Mammals'. Palaeozoic: 'Age of Fishes and Amphibians'.","src":["TGT 24 Jun 2023 S3"]}]},{"id":"physical","n":"Physical Geography","qs":[{"id":"q0154","q":"The Inter-Tropical Convergence Zone (ITCZ) shifts in which of the following directions during the summer season?","o":["South","East","North","West"],"a":2,"x":"In summer the ITCZ moves north with the overhead Sun. Over India it reaches about 20–25°N (the monsoon trough), pulling in the south-west monsoon.","e":"In winter it moves south of the equator.","src":["ASO 19 Aug 2025 S2"]},{"id":"q0193","q":"Which of the following processes is most responsible for the formation of exfoliation domes in granitic terrains?","o":["Chemical weathering","Biological activity","Freeze-thaw action","Pressure release"],"a":3,"x":"Exfoliation domes form by pressure release (unloading): as overlying rock erodes away, granite expands and peels off in curved sheets.","e":"Example: Half Dome in Yosemite, USA.","src":["ASO 19 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0270","q":"Which of the following factors influences the temperature and rainfall pattern of a place?","o":["Area","Altitude","Population density","Ocean depth"],"a":1,"x":"Altitude affects temperature and rainfall: temperature falls about 6.5°C for every 1,000 m of height.","e":"That is why Shimla is cooler than Delhi in summer.","src":["ASO 20 Aug 2025 S2"]},{"id":"q0315","q":"Which of the following factors is NOT a primary influence on the formation and direction of ocean currents?","o":["Salinity gradients","Wind patterns","Temperature differentials","Lunar phases"],"a":3,"x":"Ocean currents are shaped by winds, differences in temperature and salinity, and Earth's rotation (Coriolis force). Lunar phases affect tides, not currents.","e":"The Gulf Stream is a warm current; the Labrador Current a cold one.","src":["ASO 20 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0347","q":"Which of the following rocks is primarily formed through the organic accumulation and compaction of plant material?","o":["Sandstone","Limestone","Stalactites","Coal"],"a":3,"x":"Coal is an organic sedimentary rock formed as buried plant matter was compacted and heated over millions of years.","e":"Stages: peat → lignite → bituminous → anthracite (highest carbon).","src":["ASO 21 Aug 2025 S2"]},{"id":"q0348","q":"Which of the following statements related to minerals is correct?","o":["They are naturally occurring substances with undefinable internal structures.","They are homogenous, naturally occurring substances with definable internal structures.","They are heterogenous, naturally occurring substances with definable internal structures.","They are heterogenous, artificially occurring substances."],"a":1,"x":"A mineral is a homogeneous, naturally occurring substance with a definite chemical composition and a definable internal structure.","e":"Rocks are combinations of minerals; granite contains quartz, feldspar and mica.","src":["ASO 21 Aug 2025 S2"]},{"id":"q0400","q":"Which of the following are formed due to deposition, accumulation and concentration in horizontal strata?","o":["Igneous rocks","Iron ores","Metamorphic rocks","Sedimentary rocks"],"a":3,"x":"Sedimentary rocks form as sediments are deposited, accumulated and compacted in horizontal layers, and minerals in them occur in beds.","e":"Examples: sandstone, shale, limestone; coal and potash also occur in beds.","src":["ASO 21 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0473","q":"The shaking energies of earthquakes of magnitude 6 and 4 on the Richter scale differ by a factor of:","o":["ten","hundred","five","two"],"a":1,"x":"The Richter scale is logarithmic: each whole number is a 10-times rise in measured shaking amplitude, so magnitudes 6 and 4 differ by 10 × 10 = 100.","e":"Energy released rises about 31.6 times per unit, so about 1,000 times between 4 and 6.","src":["ASO 27 Aug 2025 S1"],"k":"In the strict sense, energy differs by about 1,000 times; DSSSB's key uses the 100-fold difference in shaking amplitude."},{"id":"q0497","q":"Which among the following types of mountains is also known as \"Horst\"?","o":["Volcanic Mountains","Fold Mountains","Block Mountains","Residual Mountains"],"a":2,"x":"Block mountains form when land between faults rises; the raised block is a horst and a sunken block is a graben (rift valley).","e":"Examples: Vosges and Black Forest (Europe); Satpura in India is often cited.","src":["Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q0697","q":"Which among the following type of mountains is also known as \"Mountains of Accumulation\"?","o":["Block Mountains","Residual Mountains","Volcanic Mountains","Fold Mountains"],"a":2,"x":"Volcanic mountains are called 'mountains of accumulation' because they build up from lava and ash piling around a vent.","e":"Examples: Mount Fuji (Japan), Mount Kilimanjaro (Tanzania).","src":["Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q1446","q":"The sinuous curves made by a river along its course are known as ______.","o":["aretes","meanders","moraines","cirques"],"a":1,"x":"Meanders are the loop-like bends a river makes in its middle and lower course; a cut-off meander becomes an oxbow lake.","e":"Aretes, cirques and moraines are glacial landforms.","src":["TGT 25 Jun 2023 S3"]}]},{"id":"india","n":"India: Relief, Rivers and Climate","qs":[{"id":"q0028","q":"The _______ is/are flanked by a stretch of narrow coastal strips, running along the Arabian Sea on the west and the Bay of Bengal on the east.","o":["Narmada river","Peninsula Plateau","Great Indian Desert","Lakshadweep Islands"],"a":1,"x":"The Peninsular Plateau is edged by the narrow Western Coastal Plains along the Arabian Sea and the broader Eastern Coastal Plains along the Bay of Bengal.","e":"The east coast is wider because big rivers (Mahanadi, Godavari, Krishna, Kaveri) build deltas there.","src":["ASO 18 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0033","q":"Which of the following originates from the Brahmagiri hills?","o":["Kaveri","Sabarmati","Narmada","Chambal"],"a":0,"x":"The Kaveri rises at Talakaveri in the Brahmagiri hills of Kodagu (Coorg), Karnataka, and reaches the Bay of Bengal through Tamil Nadu.","e":"Don't confuse it with the Godavari, which rises near Trimbakeshwar (Nashik) — also on a hill called Brahmagiri.","src":["ASO 18 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0065","q":"Select the correct pair of rivers which flow into the Bay of Bengal.","o":["Beas and Yamuna","Krishna and Kosi","Tapi and Mahanadi","Mahi and Damodar"],"a":1,"x":"The Krishna empties into the Bay of Bengal directly; the Kosi reaches it through the Ganga. Every other pair has a river that drains to the Arabian Sea or into the Ganga.","e":"West-flowing rivers to the Arabian Sea: Narmada, Tapi, Mahi, Sabarmati.","src":["ASO 18 Aug 2025 S2"],"k":"The Kosi is a tributary of the Ganga, so this pair is correct only in the sense of draining to the Bay of Bengal."},{"id":"q0066","q":"What is the local name of the calcareous soil deposit found in the upper part of the Ganga-Brahmaputra flood plains?","o":["Regur","Kankar","Khadar","Lal Mati"],"a":1,"x":"Kankar are calcareous (lime) nodules found in the older alluvium of the Ganga plains, in the drier upper parts away from the fresh floodplains.","e":"Bhangar = older alluvium away from rivers; khadar = new alluvium on the floodplains.","src":["ASO 18 Aug 2025 S2"]},{"id":"q0073","q":"What is the total length of the coastline of India's mainland and island groups?","o":["7517 Kilometres","7577 Kilometres","7751 Kilometres","7157 Kilometres"],"a":0,"x":"India's traditional coastline figure, mainland plus islands, is 7,516.6 km (about 7,517 km), touching 9 states and 4 UTs.","e":"Gujarat has the longest mainland coastline.","src":["ASO 18 Aug 2025 S2"],"k":"A 2023-24 re-measurement with new methods puts the coastline at 11,098 km; exam keys set before that use 7,517 km."},{"id":"q0110","q":"Read the statements regarding the Ghaghara river. A. It originates from the glaciers of Mapchachungo. B. Tila, Seti and Beri are tributaries of the Ghaghara.","o":["Both A and B are correct.","Both A and B are incorrect.","Only A is correct.","Only B is correct."],"a":0,"x":"Both are right: the Ghaghara rises in the glaciers of Mapchachungo, and collects the Tila, Seti and Beri before leaving the mountains at Shishapani.","e":"The Ghaghara joins the Ganga near Chhapra in Bihar.","src":["ASO 18 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0144","q":"Which of the following lie(s) to the north of the Narmada river and covers a major area of the Malwa Plateau?","o":["The Central Highlands","Eastern Ghats","Western Ghats","Deccan Plateau"],"a":0,"x":"The Central Highlands lie north of the Narmada and cover most of the Malwa Plateau; the Deccan Plateau lies south of the Narmada.","e":"Rivers of the Central Highlands (Chambal, Betwa, Ken) flow north-east.","src":["ASO 19 Aug 2025 S2"]},{"id":"q0189","q":"Which of the following is locally known as 'Mahawat' in the northern and northern plains of India?","o":["Cloudburst during the monsoon season","Cyclonic rainfall during winter season","Hot and dry winds during summer season","Cold waves during winter season"],"a":1,"x":"'Mahawat' is the local name for the light winter rain in north and north-west India from cyclonic western disturbances; it helps rabi crops such as wheat.","e":"The disturbances come from the Mediterranean region.","src":["ASO 19 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0197","q":"The Manasbal lake is located in which of the following Union Territories of India?","o":["Jammu and Kashmir","Ladakh","Chandigarh","Andaman and Nicobar Islands"],"a":0,"x":"Manasbal Lake is in Ganderbal district of Jammu and Kashmir and is known for lotus blooms and birdlife.","e":"Other J&K lakes: Dal, Wular (largest freshwater lake), Anchar. Ladakh has Pangong Tso and Tso Moriri.","src":["ASO 19 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0200","q":"Which of the following weather conditions is/are typical during the cold weather season in north India?","o":["Heavy rainfall","Clear sky, low temperatures, and low humidity","Hot and humid weather","Thunderstorms"],"a":1,"x":"North India's cold weather season (December–February) has clear skies, low temperatures, low humidity and light winds.","e":"The exception is light rain from western disturbances.","src":["ASO 19 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0231","q":"Which of the following contributes to most of the rainfall in India?","o":["Western Disturbances","Northeast Monsoon","Southwest Monsoon","Retreating Monsoon"],"a":2,"x":"The south-west monsoon (June–September) brings about 75% of India's annual rainfall.","e":"The north-east (retreating) monsoon matters mainly for Tamil Nadu's Coromandel coast.","src":["ASO 20 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0264","q":"What is the average temperature range in Chennai during the cold weather season?","o":["24°C–25°C","30°C–35°C","10°C–15°C","15°C–20°C"],"a":0,"x":"Chennai stays warm in the cold season, around 24–25°C, because the sea moderates its temperature.","e":"In the same season the north-western plains average 10–15°C.","src":["ASO 20 Aug 2025 S2"]},{"id":"q0302","q":"What are the strong, gusty, hot, dry daytime winds of north and north-west India called?","o":["Westerlies","Easterlies","Loo","Monsoon winds"],"a":2,"x":"The loo is the strong, gusty, hot and dry daytime wind of north and north-west India in May–June.","e":"Pre-monsoon showers elsewhere: 'mango showers' (Kerala, Karnataka), 'Kal Baisakhi' (Bengal, Assam).","src":["ASO 20 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0310","q":"Which of the following states of India does NOT experience 'Loo' in the summer?","o":["Haryana","Punjab","Telangana","Rajasthan"],"a":2,"x":"The loo blows over the north-western plains: Punjab, Haryana, Rajasthan, Uttar Pradesh, Delhi and Bihar. Telangana, on the Deccan Plateau, does not get it.","e":"Rajasthan's Thar Desert is where the loo is hottest.","src":["ASO 20 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0319","q":"Which statement about tropical rainforests is correct?","o":["They are found in areas with less than 70 cm of rain.","Their trees shed leaves according to season.","They are found in areas with more than 200 cm of rain.","They experience a long dry season every year."],"a":2,"x":"Tropical evergreen rainforests grow where rain exceeds 200 cm a year, such as the Western Ghats, the north-east, and the Andaman and Nicobar Islands.","e":"They stay green all year: rosewood, mahogany, ebony.","src":["ASO 20 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0354","q":"What kind of vegetation is found in the desert of Rajasthan?","o":["Tropical Deciduous Forests","Mangrove Forests","Montane Forests","Tropical Thorn Forests and Scrubs"],"a":3,"x":"The Rajasthan desert has tropical thorn forests and scrub: acacia, khejri, cactus and babool, suited to less than 70 cm of rain.","e":"Plants there have long roots and thick, small leaves to save water.","src":["ASO 21 Aug 2025 S2"]},{"id":"q0355","q":"Which of the following are the tributaries of the Indus river system?","o":["Ganga, Ken and Betwa","Shyok, Gilgit and Hunza","Chenab, Jhelum and Yamuna","Beas, Satluj and Ravi"],"a":1,"x":"The Shyok, Gilgit and Hunza join the Indus in its upper, mountain course. The Beas, Satluj and Ravi join through the Panjnad.","e":"The Indus rises near Lake Mansarovar in Tibet.","src":["ASO 21 Aug 2025 S2"],"k":"The Beas, Satluj and Ravi are also part of the Indus system; DSSSB's key marks the Shyok–Gilgit–Hunza set."},{"id":"q0383","q":"Which of the following is made up of fresh newer alluvium that is deposited by the rivers flowing down from the Himalayas to the plain?","o":["Khadar land","Terai land","Bangar land","Bhabar land"],"a":0,"x":"Khadar is the fresh, newer alluvium that rivers from the Himalayas deposit on their floodplains every year; it is very fertile.","e":"Bhangar is older alluvium on higher ground; Bhabar is a pebble belt at the foothills; Terai is the swampy belt south of Bhabar.","src":["ASO 21 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0384","q":"In which state are the Karbi Anglong hills located?","o":["Karnataka","Odisha","Maharashtra","Assam"],"a":3,"x":"The Karbi Anglong hills are in central Assam, part of the Meghalaya (Shillong) plateau extension.","e":"Karbi Anglong is a Sixth Schedule autonomous district.","src":["ASO 21 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0394","q":"What kind of planetary winds prevail over India during the cold weather season?","o":["Northeast trade winds","The polar easterlies","Southwest trade winds","The westerlies"],"a":0,"x":"In the cold weather season, north-east trade winds blow over India from land to sea, so most of the country stays dry.","e":"They pick up moisture over the Bay of Bengal and bring winter rain to Tamil Nadu.","src":["ASO 21 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0397","q":"Which of the following Indian rivers has its source in the Amarkantak plateau and flows into the Arabian Sea?","o":["Krishna","Godavari","Kaveri","Narmada"],"a":3,"x":"The Narmada rises at Amarkantak (Madhya Pradesh) and flows west through a rift valley into the Arabian Sea; the Son also rises at Amarkantak but flows east.","e":"The Tapi is the other big west-flowing rift-valley river.","src":["ASO 21 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0424","q":"The famous hill stations 'Dharamshala' and 'Mussoorie' are located in which range of the Himalayas?","o":["Shiwalik Range","Lesser Himalaya Range","Trans Himalaya Range","Greater Himalaya Range"],"a":1,"x":"Dharamshala, Mussoorie, Shimla, Nainital and Darjeeling are in the Lesser (Middle) Himalaya, the Himachal range.","e":"Shiwaliks are the outermost range; the Greater Himalaya (Himadri) holds the highest peaks.","src":["ASO 22 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0433","q":"In the physiography of India, the Palkonda range is a part of ______.","o":["Himalayan ranges","Vindhya hills","Eastern Ghats","Western Ghats"],"a":2,"x":"The Palkonda range of Andhra Pradesh is part of the Eastern Ghats, which are broken and discontinuous.","e":"Other Eastern Ghats hills: Javadi, Shevaroy, Nallamala. The highest peak is Mahendragiri (Odisha) or Arma Konda (Andhra Pradesh).","src":["ASO 22 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0434","q":"Where do western disturbances originate?","o":["Bay of Bengal","Mediterranean Sea","Indian Ocean","Arabian Sea"],"a":1,"x":"Western disturbances are low-pressure systems that start over the Mediterranean Sea and move east on the westerly jet stream to bring winter rain and snow to north India.","e":"The rain helps the wheat crop.","src":["ASO 22 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0438","q":"Identify the place of origin of the Betwa river.","o":["Himalayas","Aravalli range","Vindhya range","Western Ghats"],"a":2,"x":"The Betwa rises in the Vindhya range near Bhopal and joins the Yamuna at Hamirpur (Uttar Pradesh).","e":"Other Yamuna tributaries from the south: Chambal, Sind, Ken.","src":["ASO 22 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0462","q":"Which of the following statements is FALSE about the Thar Desert?","o":["The eastern part of the Thar Desert is known as the Rajasthan Bagar.","The Luni flows towards the north-east to the Rann of Kachchh.","The tract north of the Luni is known as thali or sandy plain.","The Thar Desert is drained by a number of short seasonal streams originating from the Aravali."],"a":1,"x":"The false statement is that the Luni flows north-east. The Luni rises in the Aravallis near Ajmer and flows south-west into the Rann of Kachchh.","e":"The Luni is salty in its lower course.","src":["ASO 27 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0463","q":"Which mountain range forms the northern border of India?","o":["Karakoram and Himalayas","Vindhya Range","Satpura Range","Aravali Range"],"a":0,"x":"The Himalayas and, in Ladakh, the Karakoram form India's northern border.","e":"K2 (Godwin Austen), in the Karakoram, is the highest peak claimed by India.","src":["ASO 27 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0464","q":"The main tributaries of the Indus River flowing in Indian Territory include all of the following EXCEPT ________.","o":["Sutlej","Tapti","Beas","Ravi"],"a":1,"x":"The Tapi (Tapti) is a west-flowing peninsular river into the Arabian Sea. The Sutlej, Beas and Ravi are Indus tributaries.","e":"The five rivers of Punjab: Jhelum, Chenab, Ravi, Beas, Sutlej.","src":["ASO 27 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0470","q":"Chambal, Sind, Betwa and Ken rivers flow through which of the following plateaus in India?","o":["Chota Nagpur Plateau","Deccan Plateau","Malwa Plateau","Meghalaya Plateau"],"a":2,"x":"The Chambal, Sind, Betwa and Ken flow north-east across the Malwa Plateau to join the Yamuna.","e":"The Malwa Plateau is made of lava (black soil) in the west.","src":["ASO 27 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q1594","q":"In 1955, with which of the following countries did India sign an agreement to construct Koshi Barrage?","o":["Sri Lanka","Nepal","China","Bangladesh"],"a":1,"x":"India and Nepal signed the Kosi Agreement in the mid-1950s for the Kosi barrage and embankments; the barrage stands at Bhimnagar on the Nepal side.","e":"The Kosi is called the 'Sorrow of Bihar'.","src":["Patwari 20 Aug 2022 S2"],"k":"The Kosi Agreement is usually dated 25 April 1954; Nepal is correct either way."},{"id":"q1614","q":"Which of the following rivers, along with their tributaries, form the Northern Plains?","o":["Indus, Yamuna and Brahmaputra","Indus, Ganga and Brahmaputra","Narmada, Ganga and Brahmaputra","Indus, Ganga and Yamuna"],"a":1,"x":"The Northern Plains were formed by three river systems and their tributaries: the Indus, Ganga and Brahmaputra.","e":"They are divided into the Punjab plains, Ganga plains and Brahmaputra plains.","src":["Patwari 17 Sep 2022 S3"]},{"id":"q1616","q":"Which Plateau generally refers to the volcanic upland north of the Vindhya Range, and Aravalli lies in the north-west of this plateau?","o":["Malwa","Bundelkhand","Baghelkhand","Deccan"],"a":0,"x":"The Malwa Plateau is the lava upland north of the Vindhyas, with the Aravallis to its north-west; it is drained by the Chambal and Betwa.","e":"The Bundelkhand and Baghelkhand uplands lie to its east.","src":["Patwari 18 Sep 2022 S1"]},{"id":"q0498","q":"Which among the following rivers was earlier known as Sadanira?","o":["Gandak","Kosi","Chambal","Betwa"],"a":0,"x":"The Gandak was known as the Sadanira ('ever-flowing') in ancient texts; it formed the border between Kosala and Videha.","e":"It rises in Nepal and joins the Ganga near Patna.","src":["Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q0535","q":"Nepal shares it's borders with which of the following Indian states?","o":["West Bengal","Mizoram","Rajasthan","Punjab"],"a":0,"x":"Nepal borders five Indian states: Uttarakhand, Uttar Pradesh, Bihar, West Bengal and Sikkim.","e":"Uttar Pradesh has the longest border with Nepal.","src":["Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q0537","q":"Which among the following rivers is also known as 'Gorakhpur's Sorrow'?","o":["West Jhelum","West Gandak","West Rapti","West Kosi"],"a":2,"x":"The West Rapti is called 'Gorakhpur's Sorrow' because of its floods in eastern Uttar Pradesh.","e":"The Kosi is 'Bihar's Sorrow'; the Damodar was 'Bengal's Sorrow'.","src":["Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q0538","q":"Eravimala, which is the highest peak located in the Anamalai Hills in the Western Ghats, is located in which of the following states?","o":["Kerala","Karnataka","Tamil Nadu","Maharashtra"],"a":0,"x":"Anamudi (2,695 m) in Kerala's Eravikulam National Park in the Anamalai hills is the highest peak of the Western Ghats and of peninsular India.","e":"Doddabetta (2,637 m) in the Nilgiris is the second highest.","src":["Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q0576","q":"Which among the following rivers is not a tributary of river Kaveri?","o":["Bhavani","Sabari","Noyyal","Kabini"],"a":1,"x":"The Sabari is a tributary of the Godavari. The Bhavani, Noyyal and Kabini are Kaveri tributaries.","e":"Other Kaveri tributaries: Hemavati, Shimsha, Arkavati, Amaravati.","src":["Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q0578","q":"Which among the following mountain peaks is not located on the Western Ghats?","o":["Kalsubai","Mullayanagiri","Anamudi","Dhupgarh"],"a":3,"x":"Dhupgarh (1,350 m) is in the Satpura range at Pachmarhi, Madhya Pradesh, not the Western Ghats. Kalsubai, Mullayanagiri and Anamudi are all in the Western Ghats.","e":"Kalsubai is Maharashtra's highest peak; Mullayanagiri is Karnataka's.","src":["Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q0615","q":"India does not share its borders with which of the following countries?","o":["China","Pakistan","Bhutan","Thailand"],"a":3,"x":"India shares land borders with Pakistan, Afghanistan, China, Nepal, Bhutan, Bangladesh and Myanmar. Thailand is a maritime neighbour only.","e":"Longest land border: with Bangladesh (4,096 km).","src":["Junior Assistant 25 Aug 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q0617","q":"Which among the following is not a tributary of Damodar river?","o":["Bokaro","Ghari","Konar","Bhavani"],"a":3,"x":"The Bhavani is a Kaveri tributary in Tamil Nadu. The Bokaro, Konar and Barakar are tributaries of the Damodar (Jharkhand–West Bengal).","e":"The Damodar Valley Corporation (1948) was India's first multipurpose river valley project.","src":["Junior Assistant 25 Aug 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q0618","q":"Which among the following separates the Tamil Nadu state of India and the Mannar district of Sri Lanka?","o":["Durand Line","Palk Strait","Radcliffe Line","MacMohan Line"],"a":1,"x":"The Palk Strait separates Tamil Nadu from the Mannar district of Sri Lanka.","e":"Adam's Bridge (Ram Setu) lies between Palk Bay and the Gulf of Mannar.","src":["Junior Assistant 25 Aug 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q0656","q":"Which among the following is the longest river in the state of Kerala?","o":["Pamba","Kallada","Periyar","Chaliyar"],"a":2,"x":"The Periyar (about 244 km) is Kerala's longest river; it feeds the Idukki dam and flows into the Arabian Sea near Kochi.","e":"The Bharathappuzha is the second longest.","src":["Junior Assistant 25 Aug 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q0657","q":"Mahendragiri is the second highest peak of Odisha located in which of the following ranges?","o":["Eastern Ghats","Western Ghats","Aravalli Range","Vindhya Range"],"a":0,"x":"Mahendragiri in Gajapati district is in the Eastern Ghats; Odisha's highest peak is Deomali.","e":"The Eastern Ghats are broken up by rivers such as the Mahanadi, Godavari and Krishna.","src":["Junior Assistant 25 Aug 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q0695","q":"Which among the following lines separates India and Pakistan?","o":["Durand Line","MacMohan Line","Hindenburg Line","Radcliffe Line"],"a":3,"x":"The Radcliffe Line, drawn by Sir Cyril Radcliffe in 1947, separates India and Pakistan (and India and Bangladesh).","e":"Durand Line: Afghanistan–Pakistan. McMahon Line: India–China, in the east.","src":["Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q0696","q":"Indravati river is the tributary of which of the following rivers?","o":["Krishna","Godavari","Narmada","Mahanadi"],"a":1,"x":"The Indravati is a tributary of the Godavari, rising in Odisha's Kalahandi and flowing through Chhattisgarh's Bastar.","e":"Chitrakote Falls on the Indravati is called the 'Niagara of India'.","src":["Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q0698","q":"Which among the following soils is formed as a result of high leaching?","o":["Laterite","Black","Red","Arid"],"a":0,"x":"Laterite soil forms in hot, heavy-rain areas where intense leaching washes away silica and lime, leaving iron and aluminium oxides.","e":"Found in the Western Ghats, Odisha and Assam; good for tea, coffee and cashew.","src":["Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q0735","q":"Amongst the following, which river was earlier known as the \"Sorrow of Bengal\"?","o":["Kosi","Damodar","Subarnarekha","Teesta"],"a":1,"x":"The Damodar was called the 'Sorrow of Bengal' because of its floods, until the Damodar Valley Corporation dams tamed it.","e":"Kosi: 'Sorrow of Bihar'. Brahmaputra: 'Sorrow of Assam'.","src":["Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q0736","q":"Which one of the following is not a Himalayan pass?","o":["Palghat pass","Banihal pass","Kora La","Zoji La"],"a":0,"x":"The Palghat (Palakkad) Gap is in the Western Ghats between the Nilgiri and Anamalai hills, linking Kerala and Tamil Nadu. Banihal, Kora La and Zoji La are Himalayan passes.","e":"Other Western Ghats passes: Thal Ghat, Bhor Ghat.","src":["Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q0738","q":"As per the Indus Waters Treaty between India and Pakistan, India has been allowed the usage of which of the following rivers?","o":["Indus, Jhelum, Chenab","Ravi, Indus, Chenab","Chenab, Jhelum, Ravi","Beas, Ravi, Sutlej"],"a":3,"x":"Under the Indus Waters Treaty (1960, brokered by the World Bank), India got the eastern rivers — Ravi, Beas and Sutlej — and Pakistan the western rivers — Indus, Jhelum and Chenab.","e":"India put the treaty 'in abeyance' in April 2025.","src":["Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q1291","q":"Which of the following rivers has the longest length?","o":["Godavari","Narmada","Krishna","Kaveri"],"a":0,"x":"Of these, the Godavari is the longest (about 1,465 km), followed by the Krishna (about 1,400 km), the Narmada (about 1,312 km) and the Kaveri (about 800 km).","e":"The Ganga (about 2,525 km) is India's longest river overall.","src":["Stenographer 19 Apr 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q1329","q":"Kalsubai is the highest peak of which of the following states?","o":["Tamil Nadu","Maharashtra","Odisha","West Bengal"],"a":1,"x":"Kalsubai (1,646 m) in Ahmednagar district is the highest peak of Maharashtra, in the Sahyadri range.","e":"Karnataka's highest: Mullayanagiri. Kerala's: Anamudi.","src":["Stenographer 23 Apr 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q1335","q":"Which among the following soils is also known as 'Usara soils'?","o":["Arid","Saline","Laterite","Alluvial"],"a":1,"x":"Saline soils are also called usar (or reh, kallar) soils: they have too much salt and are infertile, and are found in arid areas and waterlogged canal regions.","e":"Adding gypsum helps treat saline soils.","src":["Stenographer 23 Apr 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q1341","q":"Which among the following states is also known as \"Orchid State of India\"?","o":["Tamil Nadu","Andhra Pradesh","Arunachal Pradesh","Madhya Pradesh"],"a":2,"x":"Arunachal Pradesh is called the 'Orchid State of India' for its hundreds of orchid species; the Orchid Research Centre is at Tipi.","e":"Sikkim is also famous for orchids.","src":["Stenographer 23 Apr 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q0983","q":"The Great Indian Plains were primarily formed by which geological process?","o":["Glacial erosion","Volcanic eruption","Sediment deposition","Tectonic uplift"],"a":2,"x":"The Great Indian (Northern) Plains were built by the deposition of alluvial sediment brought by the Indus, Ganga and Brahmaputra systems into a trough (foredeep) south of the Himalayas.","e":"The plains cover about 7 lakh sq km.","src":["PRT 19 Feb 2026 S1"]},{"id":"q1048","q":"Which of the following options correctly matches the southernmost points of India?","o":["Southernmost island point – Kanyakumari","Southernmost point of Union – Munnar","Southernmost mainland point – Kanyakumari","Southernmost mainland point – Minicoy Island"],"a":2,"x":"Kanyakumari (Cape Comorin) is the southernmost point of mainland India; Indira Point on Great Nicobar is the southernmost point of India's territory.","e":"Northernmost: Indira Col. Westernmost: Guhar Moti (Gujarat). Easternmost: Kibithu (Arunachal).","src":["PRT 20 Feb 2026 S1"]},{"id":"q1050","q":"The deltaic plain in the eastern part of the Great Indian Plains is primarily formed by:","o":["Ganga and Brahmaputra rivers","Ghaggar and Sutlej rivers","Yamuna and Chambal rivers","Narmada and Tapi rivers"],"a":0,"x":"The eastern deltaic plain of the Great Plains is formed by the Ganga and Brahmaputra; the Sundarbans delta is the world's largest.","e":"The Sundarbans is a UNESCO site known for the Royal Bengal tiger.","src":["PRT 20 Feb 2026 S1"]},{"id":"q1114","q":"Which region of the Great Indian Plains is known for the presence of newer alluvium (khadar)?","o":["Rajasthan plain","Riverine tracts along the Yamuna and Ganga","Punjab plain","Ganga delta"],"a":3,"x":"Khadar is newer alluvium laid down on floodplains every year. DSSSB's key marks the Ganga delta, where fresh alluvium keeps building up.","e":"Bhangar is the older alluvium above flood level.","src":["PRT 23 Feb 2026 S3"],"k":"The floodplains along the Yamuna and Ganga are also classic khadar; the key prefers the delta."},{"id":"q1181","q":"Which of the following statements correctly describes the Great Indian Desert?","o":["It is located in the northeastern part of India and receives high rainfall","It lies to the east of the Aravalli Hills and has fertile alluvial soil","It is a hot desert located in northwestern India with arid climate and sandy soil","It is formed by volcanic eruptions and is rich in black soil"],"a":2,"x":"The Great Indian (Thar) Desert is a hot desert in north-western India, west of the Aravallis, with an arid climate, sandy soil and less than 150 mm of rain in places.","e":"The Luni is the only large river in the region.","src":["PRT 31 Mar 2026 S1"]},{"id":"q1185","q":"Which island feature is found exclusively in the Lakshadweep group but not in the Andaman and Nicobar group?","o":["Indigenous tribal settlements","Atoll formations","Volcanic activity","Tropical evergreen forests"],"a":1,"x":"Lakshadweep is made of coral atolls. The Andaman and Nicobar Islands are mostly the tops of submerged mountains, with volcanoes such as Barren Island, and have tribes and evergreen forests.","e":"Barren Island is India's only active volcano.","src":["PRT 31 Mar 2026 S1"]},{"id":"q1196","q":"By what popular name is the Godavari river known?","o":["Padma Ganga","Ramganga","Dev Ganga","Dakshin Ganga"],"a":3,"x":"The Godavari, India's second-longest river, is called the 'Dakshin Ganga' (Ganga of the South); it rises near Trimbakeshwar, Nashik.","e":"Its tributaries include the Pranhita, Indravati, Manjira and Sabari.","src":["PRT 31 Mar 2026 S2"]},{"id":"q1199","q":"Which river is antecedent in origin and has cut deep gorges through the Himalayas?","o":["Chambal","Yamuna","Son","Indus"],"a":3,"x":"The Indus is an antecedent river: it is older than the Himalayas and kept cutting down as they rose, carving deep gorges.","e":"The Sutlej and Brahmaputra are antecedent too.","src":["PRT 31 Mar 2026 S2"]},{"id":"q1211","q":"Which statement about the coastal plains of India is correct?","o":["The Western Coastal Plain is wide and has deltas","The Western Coastal Plain is broad and has lagoons","The Eastern Coastal Plain is narrow and has estuaries","The Eastern Coastal Plain is broad and has delta formations"],"a":3,"x":"The Eastern Coastal Plain is broad and has large deltas (Mahanadi, Godavari, Krishna, Kaveri). The Western Coastal Plain is narrow, with estuaries and lagoons (Kerala's backwaters).","e":"Chilika Lake (Odisha) is India's largest brackish-water lagoon.","src":["PRT 31 Mar 2026 S2"]},{"id":"q1224","q":"The Lakshadweep islands are mainly made up of _____________.","o":["Volcanic rocks","Coral islands","Granite rocks","Sand dunes"],"a":1,"x":"The Lakshadweep islands are coral islands (atolls and reefs) in the Arabian Sea.","e":"The Andaman and Nicobar Islands are mainly submerged mountain peaks, with some volcanoes.","src":["PRT 31 Mar 2026 S3"]},{"id":"q1441","q":"'Bangar' and 'Khadar' are sub-types of which soil?","o":["Black soil","Alluvial soil","Red soil","Laterite soil"],"a":1,"x":"Bhangar (older alluvium) and khadar (newer alluvium) are the two types of alluvial soil in the Northern Plains.","e":"Alluvial soil covers about 40% of India's land.","src":["TGT 25 Jun 2023 S2"]},{"id":"q1467","q":"The Narmada originates from which of the following places?","o":["Amarkantak plateau","Mahabaleshwar","Brahmagiri hills","Chotanagpur Plateau"],"a":0,"x":"The Narmada rises at the Amarkantak plateau in Anuppur district, Madhya Pradesh.","e":"The Krishna rises at Mahabaleshwar; the Kaveri in the Brahmagiri hills.","src":["TGT 7 Feb 2024 S2"]},{"id":"q1482","q":"Which of these hills is NOT included in the Purvanchal or Eastern hills?","o":["Manipur hills","Shevroy Hills","Patkai hills","Naga hills"],"a":1,"x":"The Shevaroy hills are in Tamil Nadu (Eastern Ghats; Yercaud). The Patkai, Naga and Manipur hills are part of the Purvanchal (Eastern hills).","e":"The Mizo (Lushai) hills are also in Purvanchal.","src":["TGT 7 Feb 2024 S2"]},{"id":"q1487","q":"Which of the following mountain ranges defines India's northern boundary?","o":["The Aravalli Range","The Himalayas","The Eastern Ghats","The Western Ghats"],"a":1,"x":"The Himalayas form India's northern boundary.","e":"The Himalayas run about 2,400 km from the Indus to the Brahmaputra.","src":["TGT 29 Aug 2025"]},{"id":"q1509","q":"Which of the following rivers marks the easternmost boundary of the Himalayas?","o":["Jhelum","Mahanadi","Brahmaputra","Godavari"],"a":2,"x":"The Himalayas are bounded by the Indus in the west and the Brahmaputra in the east.","e":"Namcha Barwa marks the eastern end, where the Brahmaputra turns south.","src":["TGT 24 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q1528","q":"According to the Köppen classification, which type of climate is found in Arunachal Pradesh?","o":["Amw – monsoon with short dry season","Dfc – cold humid winter with short summer","BShw – semi-arid steppe climate","Cwg – monsoon with dry winter"],"a":1,"x":"In NCERT's Köppen classification of India, Arunachal Pradesh has the Dfc type: a cold, humid winter with a short summer.","e":"Amw (monsoon with short dry season): the west coast south of Goa. BWhw (hot desert): western Rajasthan.","src":["TGT 24 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q1548","q":"Jelep La and Nathu La are on the main Indo-Tibet trade route through which valley?","o":["Damodar Valley","Chumbi Valley","Ketti Valley","Gurez Valley"],"a":1,"x":"Jelep La and Nathu La, in Sikkim, lie on the old Indo-Tibet trade route through the Chumbi Valley.","e":"Nathu La reopened for India–China border trade in 2006.","src":["TGT 31 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0763","q":"Which of the following statements about the Lakshadweep islands is correct?","o":["A dormant volcano named Narcondam is located in Lakshadweep.","Kavaratti island is the administrative headquarters of Lakshadweep.","The Lakshadweep islands are located in the Bay of Bengal.","Wheeler Island is a part of the Lakshadweep group."],"a":1,"x":"Kavaratti is the administrative headquarters of Lakshadweep, in the Arabian Sea. Narcondam volcano is in the Andamans (Bay of Bengal), and Wheeler (Abdul Kalam) Island is off Odisha.","e":"Minicoy is Lakshadweep's southernmost and second-largest island.","src":["PGT 25 Sep 2025"]},{"id":"q0781","q":"Which of the following pairs of rivers in India and their sources is correctly matched?","o":["Indus – Nasik","Ganga – Gangotri Glacier","Brahmaputra- Amarkantak Plateau","Godavari – Siachen Glacier"],"a":1,"x":"The Ganga rises as the Bhagirathi from the Gangotri Glacier (Gaumukh) in Uttarakhand. The Indus rises in Tibet, the Brahmaputra near Mansarovar, and the Godavari near Nashik.","e":"The Bhagirathi and Alaknanda meet at Devprayag to form the Ganga.","src":["PGT 11 Jul 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0814","q":"India primarily experiences which type of climate?","o":["Monsoon climate","Equatorial climate","Tundra climate","Mediterranean climate"],"a":0,"x":"India has a tropical monsoon climate: seasonal reversal of winds and most rain in the south-west monsoon months.","e":"Summers are hot and winters relatively dry, with regional variation.","src":["PGT 22 Sep 2025"]},{"id":"q0835","q":"Which among the following is NOT a characteristic of peninsular rivers of India?","o":["Most of the major peninsular rivers except Narmada and Tapti flow from west to east.","The Mahanadi rises near Sihawa in Raipur district of Chhattisgarh.","Most of the major peninsular rivers except Narmada and Tapti flow from east to west.","The Peninsular drainage system is older than the Himalayan."],"a":2,"x":"The false statement says most peninsular rivers flow east to west. Most (Mahanadi, Godavari, Krishna, Kaveri) flow west to east into the Bay of Bengal; the Narmada and Tapi flow west.","e":"The Peninsular drainage is older than the Himalayan.","src":["PGT 26 Sep 2025"]}]},{"id":"resources","n":"Resources, Agriculture and Industry","qs":[{"id":"q0029","q":"According to India’s Water Bodies Census 2018-19, which of the following types of water bodies accounts for the highest proportion in India?","o":["Tanks","Reservoirs","Ponds","Lakes"],"a":2,"x":"India's first Water Bodies Census (2018-19) counted about 24.2 lakh water bodies. Ponds were the largest share (about 59.5%), then tanks and reservoirs.","e":"West Bengal has the most water bodies; Tamil Nadu has the most lakes; Andhra Pradesh the most tanks.","src":["ASO 18 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0038","q":"According to India’s Water Bodies Census 2018-19, which state reported the highest number of lakes?","o":["Kerala","Karnataka","Tamil Nadu","West Bengal"],"a":2,"x":"The Water Bodies Census 2018-19 found Tamil Nadu has the most lakes, while West Bengal has the most water bodies overall (mostly ponds).","e":"Andhra Pradesh leads in tanks.","src":["ASO 18 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0102","q":"Which of the following pairs of agencies involved in mineral exploration and their headquarters is correct?","o":["Geological Survey of India (GSI) – Kolkata","Oil and Natural Gas Corporation (ONGC) – Jaipur","Mineral Exploration Corporation Ltd. (MECL) – Chennai","National Mineral Development Corporation (NMDC) – Bhopal"],"a":0,"x":"The Geological Survey of India (1851) has its headquarters in Kolkata. The other pairs are wrong: ONGC is in New Delhi (registered at Dehradun), MECL in Nagpur, NMDC in Hyderabad.","e":"GSI was set up mainly to find coal for the railways.","src":["ASO 18 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0113","q":"India has the biggest reserve of _______ in the Koderma district of Jharkhand.","o":["iron ore","mica","coal","manganese"],"a":1,"x":"The Koderma belt of Jharkhand holds India's biggest mica reserves; mica is used in electrical and electronic insulation.","e":"Other mica areas: Nellore (Andhra Pradesh) and Bhilwara–Ajmer (Rajasthan).","src":["ASO 18 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0145","q":"In which of the following years was India's first water bodies census conducted?","o":["2015-16","2021-22","2018-19","2011-12"],"a":2,"x":"India's first Water Bodies Census was carried out in 2018-19 with the 6th Minor Irrigation Census; the report came out in 2023.","e":"It counted about 24.2 lakh water bodies.","src":["ASO 19 Aug 2025 S2"]},{"id":"q0179","q":"In which of the following years was the foundation stone for the Visakhapatnam Steel Plant laid?","o":["1957","1981","1971","1966"],"a":2,"x":"Indira Gandhi laid the foundation stone of the Visakhapatnam Steel Plant in January 1971 after the 'Visakha Ukku, Andhrula Hakku' agitation.","e":"It is India's first shore-based integrated steel plant (RINL).","src":["ASO 19 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0198","q":"The North Karanpura and South Karanpura coal reserves are located in which State?","o":["Jharkhand","West Bengal","Chhattisgarh","Andhra Pradesh"],"a":0,"x":"North and South Karanpura coalfields are in the Damodar valley of Jharkhand.","e":"Jharia is India's main coking-coal field; Raniganj, the first to be worked, is in West Bengal.","src":["ASO 19 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0239","q":"Which of the following is a section of a dam through which it is intended that water will flow either intermittently or continuously?","o":["Basin","Weir","Guls","Kuls"],"a":1,"x":"A weir is a low barrier across a river over which water flows continuously or at intervals; it raises the level upstream for irrigation or diversion.","e":"A dam stores water; a weir mostly controls the level.","src":["ASO 20 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0381","q":"Man-made water bodies that were traditionally created to allow boats and other vessels to travel between two major water bodies are known as:","o":["ports","lagoons","canals","brooks"],"a":2,"x":"Canals are artificial waterways, traditionally dug so boats could travel between two water bodies.","e":"Suez Canal: Mediterranean to Red Sea. Panama Canal: Atlantic to Pacific.","src":["ASO 21 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q1597","q":"Which of the following is a non-traditional energy source?","o":["Petroleum","Coal","Natural Gas","Wind"],"a":3,"x":"Wind is a non-traditional (non-conventional, renewable) energy source. Petroleum, coal and natural gas are conventional fossil fuels.","e":"Tamil Nadu and Gujarat lead India in wind power.","src":["Patwari 17 Sep 2022 S2"]},{"id":"q1599","q":"India's first fully organic state is ______.","o":["Meghalaya","Nagaland","Sikkim","Tripura"],"a":2,"x":"Sikkim was declared India's first fully organic state in January 2016.","e":"Sikkim won the UN FAO's Future Policy Gold Award in 2018.","src":["Patwari 17 Sep 2022 S2"]},{"id":"q1602","q":"Which of the following minerals is not metallic?","o":["Copper","Silver","Gold","Limestone"],"a":3,"x":"Limestone is a non-metallic mineral, mainly calcium carbonate, used for cement. Copper, silver and gold are metallic.","e":"Other non-metallic minerals: mica, gypsum, salt.","src":["Patwari 17 Sep 2022 S2"]},{"id":"q1609","q":"In which state of India is a geothermal power plant located?","o":["Assam","Tripura","Nagaland","Himachal Pradesh"],"a":3,"x":"Himachal Pradesh's Manikaran (Parbati valley) has hot springs where India's geothermal energy potential was explored with a pilot plant.","e":"Puga valley in Ladakh is India's most promising geothermal site.","src":["Patwari 17 Sep 2022 S3"]},{"id":"q0490","q":"Which among the following is not an example of abiotic natural resource?","o":["Iron","Coal","Silver","Gold"],"a":1,"x":"Coal forms from buried plant matter, so it is counted as a biotic resource. Iron, silver and gold are abiotic.","e":"Petroleum is biotic for the same reason.","src":["Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q0530","q":"Which among the following is not an exhaustible resource?","o":["Natural gas","Petroleum","Biogas","Coal"],"a":2,"x":"Biogas is renewable because it is made continuously from cattle dung and organic waste. Natural gas, petroleum and coal are exhaustible fossil fuels.","e":"Biogas is mostly methane.","src":["Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q0611","q":"Which among the following is the first thermal power plant of National Thermal Power Corporation Limited?","o":["Talcher Super Thermal Power Station","Sikka Thermal Power Plant","Rihand Thermal Power Plant","Singrauli Super Thermal Power Plant"],"a":3,"x":"Singrauli Super Thermal Power Station in Uttar Pradesh was NTPC's first power project (first unit 1982).","e":"NTPC was set up in 1975; it is a Maharatna company.","src":["Junior Assistant 25 Aug 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q0650","q":"Which among the following is an example of non-conventional source of energy?","o":["Oil","Coal","Bioenergy","Natural Gas"],"a":2,"x":"Bioenergy (from biomass, biogas, biofuels) is a non-conventional, renewable source. Oil, coal and natural gas are conventional fossil fuels.","e":"Other non-conventional sources: solar, wind, tidal, geothermal.","src":["Junior Assistant 25 Aug 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q0737","q":"As of April 2020, which state produces the maximum quantity of coffee in India?","o":["Karnataka","Odisha","Tamil Nadu","Kerala"],"a":0,"x":"Karnataka produces about 70% of India's coffee, mainly in Kodagu, Chikkamagaluru and Hassan.","e":"Coffee first came to India at Baba Budan Giri in Chikkamagaluru.","src":["Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q0903","q":"Match List I (Industry) with List II (Location): A. Visvesvaraya Iron and Steel Plant, B. Indian Aluminium Company Ltd., C. Fertiliser Corporation of India Ltd., D. Hindustan Antibiotics Ltd. — I. Hirakud, II. Sindri, III. Pimpri, IV. Bhadravati.","o":["A - IV, B - I, C - II, D - III","A - II, B - IV, C - I, D - III","A - IV, B - II, C - I, D - III","A - I, B - II, C - III, D - IV"],"a":0,"x":"Visvesvaraya Iron and Steel Plant – Bhadravati (Karnataka); Indian Aluminium Company – Hirakud (Odisha); Fertiliser Corporation of India – Sindri (Jharkhand); Hindustan Antibiotics – Pimpri (Pune).","e":"Sindri was India's first large fertiliser plant (1951).","src":["PRT 17 Feb 2026 S3"]},{"id":"q0922","q":"On the map of India, where is the Korba Thermal Power Plant located?","o":["Chhattisgarh","Maharashtra","Jharkhand","Odisha"],"a":0,"x":"The Korba super thermal power plant (NTPC) is in Korba district, Chhattisgarh, on the Hasdeo river near large coalfields.","e":"Korba is called the 'power capital of India'.","src":["PRT 18 Feb 2026 S1"]},{"id":"q0932","q":"Which of the following is NOT a major iron ore belt of India?","o":["Singhbhum belt","Ratnagiri–Mumbai","Ballari–Chitradurga","Durg–Bastar–Chandrapur"],"a":1,"x":"NCERT's major iron ore belts: Odisha–Jharkhand (Singhbhum), Durg–Bastar–Chandrapur, Ballari–Chitradurga–Chikkamagaluru–Tumakuru, and Maharashtra–Goa. 'Ratnagiri–Mumbai' is not one of them.","e":"Kudremukh (Karnataka) was a famous iron ore export mine.","src":["PRT 18 Feb 2026 S1"]},{"id":"q1110","q":"According to the Indian Minerals Yearbook 2022, which state is the leading producer of bauxite in India?","o":["Jharkhand","Odisha","Karnataka","Maharashtra"],"a":1,"x":"Odisha is India's leading bauxite producer (Koraput, Kalahandi, Rayagada); bauxite is the ore of aluminium.","e":"NALCO's plants are at Damanjodi and Angul, Odisha.","src":["PRT 23 Feb 2026 S3"]},{"id":"q1215","q":"Which industrial region of India is known for a concentration of heavy industries?","o":["Delhi–Gurgaon","Bengaluru–Tamil Nadu","Mumbai–Pune","Chota Nagpur Plateau"],"a":3,"x":"The Chota Nagpur Plateau (Jharkhand, West Bengal, Odisha) has heavy industry — iron and steel at Jamshedpur, Bokaro and Durgapur — because coal and iron ore are close by.","e":"It is called the 'Ruhr of India'.","src":["PRT 31 Mar 2026 S3"]},{"id":"q1436","q":"Which state has major reserves of low-grade brown coal called lignite, which is used for power generation?","o":["Gujarat","West Bengal","Odisha","Tamil Nadu"],"a":3,"x":"Tamil Nadu has India's largest lignite (brown coal) reserves, at Neyveli, used for power generation.","e":"Lignite is also mined in Gujarat and Rajasthan.","src":["TGT 25 Jun 2023 S2"]},{"id":"q1520","q":"Which of the following crops requires a hot and humid climate in the Indian climatic condition, with an average temperature of 21°C to 27°C?","o":["Wheat","Sugarcane","Gram","Mustard"],"a":1,"x":"Sugarcane needs a hot, humid climate (21–27°C) and 75–100 cm of rain; Uttar Pradesh is the largest producer.","e":"Wheat, gram and mustard are cool-season rabi crops.","src":["TGT 24 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0768","q":"In which state are the Kolihan copper mines located?","o":["Madhya Pradesh","Jharkhand","Rajasthan","Andhra Pradesh"],"a":2,"x":"The Kolihan copper mine is part of the Khetri copper belt in Jhunjhunu district, Rajasthan, run by Hindustan Copper Limited.","e":"Other copper areas: Malanjkhand (Madhya Pradesh), Singhbhum (Jharkhand).","src":["PGT 25 Sep 2025"]},{"id":"q0777","q":"Which of the following is a rabi crop of the northern states of India?","o":["Cotton","Maize","Gram","Rice"],"a":2,"x":"Gram (chickpea) is a rabi crop, sown in winter (October–December) and harvested in spring. Cotton, maize and rice are kharif crops.","e":"Other rabi crops: wheat, barley, peas, mustard.","src":["PGT 25 Sep 2025"]},{"id":"q0805","q":"What was the major objective behind the launch of National Highway Development Programme (NHDP) in India in the year 1998?","o":["To reduce rural-urban migration","To strengthen coastal protection","To boost economic development through road connectivity","To provide employment in the agricultural sector"],"a":2,"x":"The National Highways Development Programme (1998) was launched to boost economic growth through better road links, including the Golden Quadrilateral and the North-South and East-West corridors.","e":"The Golden Quadrilateral links Delhi, Mumbai, Chennai and Kolkata.","src":["PGT 22 Sep 2025"]},{"id":"q0811","q":"Which of the following states in India is a major producer of iron ore?","o":["Kerala","Uttarakhand","Punjab","Odisha"],"a":3,"x":"Odisha is India's largest producer of iron ore, from mines in Keonjhar, Sundargarh and Mayurbhanj.","e":"Karnataka and Chhattisgarh follow.","src":["PGT 22 Sep 2025"]},{"id":"q0824","q":"Which of the following term defines an association between national security and the availability of natural resources for energy consumption?","o":["Energy Security","Forest Security","Foreign Security","Mineral Security"],"a":0,"x":"Energy security is having reliable, affordable access to energy sources, which is tied to national security.","e":"India imports over 80% of its crude oil, which is why energy security matters.","src":["PGT 26 Sep 2025"]},{"id":"q0831","q":"Which is the largest (highest capacity) nuclear plant in India?","o":["Muppandal","Mundra","Kudankulam","Vindhyachal"],"a":2,"x":"Kudankulam in Tamil Nadu, built with Russia, is India's largest nuclear power plant by capacity.","e":"Tarapur (1969) was India's first nuclear power plant.","src":["PGT 26 Sep 2025"]},{"id":"q0843","q":"What percentage of the world’s water resources does India account for?","o":["4 per cent","6 per cent","3 per cent","9 per cent"],"a":0,"x":"India has about 2.45% of the world's land area and about 4% of its water resources, but about 18% of its population.","e":"Hence the pressure on water per person.","src":["PGT 14 Jul 2025"]}]},{"id":"people","n":"Population and Census","qs":[{"id":"q0035","q":"According to Census of India 2011, which state recorded the lowest female literacy rate?","o":["Odisha","Madhya Pradesh","Rajasthan","Chhattisgarh"],"a":2,"x":"Census 2011: Rajasthan had the lowest female literacy (about 52%), and Kerala the highest (about 92%).","e":"Lowest overall literacy in 2011: Bihar (about 62%).","src":["ASO 18 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0039","q":"As per NFHS 2015, which religion has the highest percentage of Scheduled Castes in India?","o":["Buddhists","Hindu","Sikhs","Christians"],"a":0,"x":"Most Indian Buddhists are Navayana (neo-Buddhists) who followed Dr B. R. Ambedkar's mass conversion of 1956, many from Scheduled Caste communities, so the SC share among Buddhists is the highest.","e":"Ambedkar converted to Buddhism at Nagpur's Deekshabhoomi on 14 October 1956.","src":["ASO 18 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0084","q":"Which of the following can be used as official languages in the Indian state of Meghalaya?","o":["English, Khasi and Garo","Meitei, Bengali and English","Hindi and English","English, Urdu and Garo"],"a":0,"x":"English is Meghalaya's official language, and Khasi and Garo are associate official languages under the Meghalaya State Language Act, 2005.","e":"Meghalaya's three hill regions are named after its peoples: Khasi, Garo and Jaintia Hills.","src":["ASO 18 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0116","q":"Which religious community forms the largest minority group in India, according to the 2011 Census?","o":["Sikhs","Muslims","Christians","Buddhists"],"a":1,"x":"Census 2011: Muslims were the largest religious minority, about 14.2% of the population.","e":"Then Christians (2.3%) and Sikhs (1.7%).","src":["ASO 18 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0153","q":"What was the main emphasis during Census 1991, regarding women's economic activities in India?","o":["Participation in community development projects","Unpaid work in family farm and family enterprise","Educational achievements","Paid work outside the household"],"a":1,"x":"The 1991 Census tried specially to record women's unpaid work on family farms and in family enterprises, which earlier censuses had largely missed.","e":"Census 2011 was India's 15th census and the 7th after Independence.","src":["ASO 19 Aug 2025 S2"]},{"id":"q0182","q":"Which of the following states has the highest population density as per the Census of 2011, excluding Delhi?","o":["Andhra Pradesh","Chhattisgarh","Assam","Bihar"],"a":3,"x":"Census 2011: Bihar had the highest population density among states, 1,106 per sq km; national average 382.","e":"Delhi (NCT) was higher, at 11,320. Lowest: Arunachal Pradesh (17).","src":["ASO 19 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0230","q":"As per the 2011 Census of India, which language had the highest number of native speakers in the state of Andhra Pradesh?","o":["Tamil","Kannada","Marathi","Telugu"],"a":3,"x":"Census 2011: Telugu had by far the most native speakers in Andhra Pradesh.","e":"Andhra State (1953) was the first state formed on a linguistic basis.","src":["ASO 20 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0266","q":"Which of following statements is INCORRECT regarding Census of India 2011?","o":["Indian Population growth percentage between 2001-2011 is 17.64%.","Percentage growth in 2001-2011 in females is 18.12 %.","Overall sex ratio at the national level has increased by 11 points.","Percentage growth in 2001-2011 in males is 17.19%."],"a":2,"x":"Census 2011 (final figures): the sex ratio rose from 933 in 2001 to 943, a rise of 10 points (provisional figures had shown 940). A rise of '11 points' is wrong; the growth percentages given are correct.","e":"Highest sex ratio: Kerala (1,084). Lowest: Haryana (879).","src":["ASO 20 Aug 2025 S2"]},{"id":"q0279","q":"According to Census 2011, which state has the highest proportion of elderly population (aged 60 years and above)?","o":["Punjab","Kerala","Maharashtra","Himachal Pradesh"],"a":1,"x":"Census 2011: Kerala had the highest proportion of elderly (60+), about 12.6%.","e":"The national figure was about 8.6%.","src":["ASO 20 Aug 2025 S2"]},{"id":"q0301","q":"As per Census 2011, identify the Union Territory with the highest male literacy rate.","o":["Chandigarh","Daman and Diu","Lakshadweep","Puducherry"],"a":2,"x":"Census 2011: Lakshadweep recorded the highest male literacy among UTs, about 96%.","e":"Highest overall literacy among states: Kerala (about 94%).","src":["ASO 20 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0357","q":"Which of the following is the largest religious minority in India as per Census 2011?","o":["Jainism","Sikhism","Christianity","Islam"],"a":3,"x":"Census 2011: Islam was the largest religious minority, about 14.2% of the population (17.22 crore).","e":"Jains are among the smallest minorities, about 0.4%.","src":["ASO 21 Aug 2025 S2"]},{"id":"q0392","q":"Which of the following union territories has the highest decadal growth in literacy rate according to Census 2011?","o":["Puducherry","Lakshadweep","Dadra and Nagar Haveli","Chandigarh"],"a":2,"x":"Among UTs, Dadra and Nagar Haveli recorded the largest decadal rise in literacy between 2001 and 2011.","e":"Highest UT literacy in 2011: Lakshadweep.","src":["ASO 21 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0398","q":"Which of the following statements is correct according to Census 2011?","o":["Punjab is the state with highest population density.","Uttarakhand is the state with lowest sex ratio.","Arunachal Pradesh is the state with lowest population density.","Puducherry is the union territory with least population."],"a":2,"x":"Census 2011: Arunachal Pradesh has the lowest population density, 17 per sq km. Bihar (not Punjab) is highest; Haryana (not Uttarakhand) has the lowest sex ratio; Lakshadweep (not Puducherry) is the least populous UT.","e":"National density: 382 per sq km.","src":["ASO 21 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0428","q":"In India, demography studies population through components such as births, deaths and _______.","o":["Marriage","Migration","Employment","Literacy"],"a":1,"x":"Population change is studied through three components: births, deaths and migration.","e":"Growth rate = birth rate − death rate ± net migration.","src":["ASO 22 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0435","q":"Demographic features in India include population size, composition, regional distribution and which component of population change?","o":["Migration","Literacy","Social mobility","Urbanisation"],"a":0,"x":"Population change has three components: births, deaths and migration. So demographic features include size, composition, distribution and migration.","e":"Urbanisation largely results from rural-to-urban migration.","src":["ASO 22 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0461","q":"The word 'Demography' is derived from which two Greek words?","o":["Demos and Logos","Geo and Graphy","Demos and Graphy","Demos and Geo"],"a":2,"x":"'Demography' comes from the Greek 'demos' (people) and 'graphein/graphy' (to describe).","e":"Demography studies population size, composition and change.","src":["ASO 27 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q1570","q":"What was the sex ratio of India in 1901?","o":["980","972","964","955"],"a":1,"x":"India's sex ratio in 1901 was 972 females per 1,000 males; it fell for most of the 20th century to 927 in 1991.","e":"It recovered to 943 in 2011.","src":["Patwari 17 Sep 2022 S3"]},{"id":"q1592","q":"As per the census 2011, what was the sex ratio (females per 1000 males) of Haryana state?","o":["841","860","901","879"],"a":3,"x":"Census 2011: Haryana had the lowest sex ratio among states, 879.","e":"Its child sex ratio (834) was also the lowest.","src":["Patwari 20 Aug 2022 S2"]},{"id":"q1619","q":"As per Census 2011 of India, density of population in India is _____ persons per sq km.","o":["382","540","456","654"],"a":0,"x":"Census 2011: India's population density was 382 persons per sq km.","e":"It was 325 in 2001.","src":["Patwari 20 Aug 2022 S1"]},{"id":"q1292","q":"What is the population density of India as per the Census 2011 (in per square km)?","o":["382","415","402","395"],"a":0,"x":"Census 2011: India's population density was 382 persons per sq km, up from 325 in 2001.","e":"Highest among states: Bihar (1,106). Lowest: Arunachal Pradesh (17).","src":["Stenographer 19 Apr 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q0909","q":"A population pyramid is used to show:","o":["Distribution of agricultural land","Urban-rural migration patterns","Age and sex structure of the population","Population density of countries"],"a":2,"x":"A population pyramid shows a population's age and sex structure: males on one side, females on the other, youngest at the bottom.","e":"A wide base means high birth rates; a narrow base means an ageing population.","src":["PRT 17 Feb 2026 S3"]},{"id":"q1266","q":"What was the sex ratio in India as reported in Census 2011?","o":["930 females per 1000 males","943 females per 1000 males","920 females per 1000 males","950 females per 1000 males"],"a":1,"x":"Census 2011 (final): 943 females per 1,000 males, up from 933 in 2001.","e":"Child sex ratio (0–6 years) fell to 919.","src":["PRT 10 Apr 2026 S1"]},{"id":"q1379","q":"The number of women who die as a result of complications from pregnancy or childbearing in a given year per 1,00,000 live births in that year is called ______.","o":["Motherly Mortality Rate (MMR)","Maternal Mortality Rate (MMR)","Mother Mortality Rate (MMR)","Maturation Mortality Rate (MMR)"],"a":1,"x":"The maternal mortality rate counts women who die from pregnancy- or childbirth-related complications per 1,00,000 live births in a year.","e":"India's MMR fell below 100 in SRS 2018–20; the SDG target is below 70 by 2030.","src":["TGT 24 Jun 2023 S2"]},{"id":"q1400","q":"As per the Census of India 2011, what percentage of Indians live in rural areas?","o":["81.2","61.5","68.8","77.3"],"a":2,"x":"Census 2011: 68.8% of Indians lived in rural areas and 31.2% in urban areas.","e":"Most urbanised large state: Tamil Nadu. Least: Himachal Pradesh.","src":["TGT 24 Jun 2023 S3"]},{"id":"q1402","q":"Which of the following states had the highest growth rate for population according to the Census of India, 2011 as compared to that of 2001?","o":["Himachal Pradesh","Meghalaya","Bihar","Uttar Pradesh"],"a":1,"x":"Of these, Meghalaya had the highest decadal population growth in 2001–11 (about 27.9%), the highest of any state; the national figure was 17.7%.","e":"Among all states and UTs, Dadra and Nagar Haveli was highest (about 55.9%).","src":["TGT 24 Jun 2023 S3"]},{"id":"q1499","q":"Which of the following age groups of women is generally taken while calculating the ‘Fertility Rate’?","o":["15 to 49 years","21 to 45 years","18 to 59 years","13 to 45 years"],"a":0,"x":"Fertility rates are calculated for women of reproductive age, 15 to 49 years.","e":"A total fertility rate of 2.1 is replacement level; India's TFR is 2.0 (NFHS-5).","src":["TGT 29 Aug 2025"]},{"id":"q1552","q":"As per Census 2011, what was the sex ratio of Kerala?","o":["1002","1084","1045","985"],"a":1,"x":"Census 2011: Kerala had the highest sex ratio among states, 1,084 females per 1,000 males.","e":"Among UTs, Puducherry was highest (1,037).","src":["TGT 31 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0800","q":"The Census Act, __________, of India states that the Census Commissioner, India, is the statutory authority vested with the responsibility of conducting the Housing and Population Census and the Rules framed thereunder.","o":["1954","1948","1949","1950"],"a":1,"x":"The Census Act, 1948 makes the Census Commissioner the statutory authority for conducting the Housing and Population Census.","e":"India's first synchronous census was in 1881; Census 2011 was the 15th.","src":["PGT 11 Jul 2025 S1"]}]},{"id":"world","n":"World and Neighbours","qs":[{"id":"q0068","q":"Which neighbouring country of India has a high proportion of people living in the Terai region, known for its fertile plains?","o":["Bhutan","Myanmar","Pakistan","Nepal"],"a":3,"x":"Nepal's Terai is the fertile, low-lying strip along the Indian border; roughly half of Nepal's people live there.","e":"Terai lies south of the Bhabar belt at the foot of the Himalayas, where streams re-emerge as marshy land.","src":["ASO 18 Aug 2025 S2"]},{"id":"q0104","q":"In which country are the Rohingya people, an ethnic minority, primarily located?","o":["Myanmar","Bangladesh","Bhutan","Nepal"],"a":0,"x":"The Rohingya are a mostly Muslim minority from Rakhine State in Myanmar; since 2017 about a million have fled to Bangladesh.","e":"Cox's Bazar in Bangladesh holds the world's largest refugee camp.","src":["ASO 18 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0226","q":"The Pashtun ethnic group is mainly concentrated in which neighbouring country of India?","o":["Bangladesh","Bhutan","Afghanistan","Nepal"],"a":2,"x":"The Pashtuns are the largest ethnic group of Afghanistan and are also numerous in Pakistan's Khyber Pakhtunkhwa.","e":"Khan Abdul Ghaffar Khan, the 'Frontier Gandhi', was a Pashtun leader.","src":["ASO 20 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0236","q":"Which commission was established to maintain liaison to ensure the most effective joint effort in maximising the benefits from common river systems between India and Bangladesh?","o":["Indo-Bangladesh Co-operation Commission","Joint Ministerial Commission on Water Resources (JMCWR)","Indo-Bangladesh Joint Rivers Commission (JRC)","Joint Consultative Commission (JCC)"],"a":2,"x":"The Indo-Bangladesh Joint Rivers Commission (1972) coordinates work on shared rivers so both countries get the most benefit.","e":"The Ganga Water Treaty was signed in 1996, for 30 years.","src":["ASO 20 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0261","q":"Which of the following is the largest ethnic group in Sri Lanka?","o":["Indian Tamils","Sinhalese","Sri Lankan Tamils","Sri Lankan Moors"],"a":1,"x":"The Sinhalese are the largest ethnic group in Sri Lanka (about three-quarters), mostly Theravada Buddhist.","e":"Sri Lankan Tamils are the largest minority.","src":["ASO 20 Aug 2025 S2"]},{"id":"q0278","q":"Maldives Islands are situated to the south of _____.","o":["Lakshadweep","Majuli","Divar Island","Andaman and Nicobar Islands"],"a":0,"x":"The Maldives lie south of Lakshadweep across the Eight Degree Channel.","e":"Nine Degree Channel: between Kavaratti (Lakshadweep) and Minicoy. Ten Degree Channel: between Little Andaman and Car Nicobar.","src":["ASO 20 Aug 2025 S2"]},{"id":"q0312","q":"The majority of Myanmar embraces which religion?","o":["Mahayana Buddhism","Theravada Buddhism","Nichiren Buddhism","Vajrayana Buddhism"],"a":1,"x":"Most people in Myanmar follow Theravada Buddhism, as in Sri Lanka, Thailand, Cambodia and Laos.","e":"Mahayana is dominant in China, Japan and Korea; Vajrayana in Tibet and Bhutan.","src":["ASO 20 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0495","q":"Which among the following is also known as \"Salt Sea\"?","o":["Dead Sea","Caspian Sea","Red Sea","Mediterranean Sea"],"a":0,"x":"The Dead Sea, between Jordan and Israel, is also called the 'Salt Sea'. It is about 430 m below sea level, the lowest land point on Earth.","e":"It is so salty that people float easily.","src":["Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q0496","q":"Mediterranean Sea is an intercontinental sea that stretches from the ____________ Ocean on the west to Asia on the east and separates Europe from Africa.","o":["Atlantic","Indian","Pacific","Arctic"],"a":0,"x":"The Mediterranean Sea stretches from the Atlantic Ocean in the west (through the Strait of Gibraltar) to Asia in the east, separating Europe from Africa.","e":"It connects to the Red Sea through the Suez Canal.","src":["Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q0536","q":"The Indian Ocean is bounded by _________ in the east.","o":["Antarctica","Australia","Africa","Iran"],"a":1,"x":"The Indian Ocean is bounded by Africa to the west, Asia to the north, Australia to the east and the Southern Ocean to the south.","e":"It is the only ocean named after a country.","src":["Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q0575","q":"Which among the following seas is the world's largest inland body of water?","o":["Black Sea","Mediterranean Sea","Caspian Sea","Red Sea"],"a":2,"x":"The Caspian Sea is the world's largest inland body of water, a saltwater lake between Europe and Asia.","e":"Largest freshwater lake by area: Lake Superior. Deepest: Lake Baikal.","src":["Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q0616","q":"Which among the following peaks is also known as \"Hidden Peak\"?","o":["Kanchenjunga","Gasherbrum I","Nanda Devi","Godwin Austen"],"a":1,"x":"Gasherbrum I (8,080 m) in the Karakoram is called 'Hidden Peak' because it is hard to see from the approach valleys.","e":"K2 is also called Godwin-Austen.","src":["Junior Assistant 25 Aug 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q0655","q":"Puerto Rico Trench is the deepest trench of which of the following oceans?","o":["Atlantic","Indian","Pacific","Arctic"],"a":0,"x":"The Puerto Rico Trench is the deepest point of the Atlantic Ocean (about 8,400 m).","e":"The Mariana Trench (Challenger Deep, about 11,000 m) in the Pacific is the deepest on Earth.","src":["Junior Assistant 25 Aug 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q0658","q":"Maldives is located to the ____________ direction of India.","o":["southeast","southwest","west","east"],"a":1,"x":"The Maldives lie to the south-west of India in the Arabian Sea, south of Lakshadweep.","e":"Sri Lanka lies to the south-east, across the Palk Strait.","src":["Junior Assistant 25 Aug 2021 S3"]}]}]});
